{"ts": "2026-08-17T10:59:24Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 163, "kind": "message", "text": "Now I have the full picture: `AddTask.jsx`, `TaskList.jsx` (with its existing `TaskRow` inline-edit pattern), `TaskFilter.jsx`'s segmented-control pattern, `App.css`'s palette, and the backend `models.py`/`store.py`. I'll design against the Taiga #4 spec content handed to me in this dispatch.\n\nOne flag before I write: `docs/spec.md` on disk still holds the prior cycle's \"Edit a task title\" content \u2014 the product-manager's Taiga #4 spec text was given inline in this dispatch pending the user's confirmation to overwrite, but hasn't actually been persisted yet. I'm treating the inline spec text as authoritative for this design (that's what I was handed to design against), and I'll note this discrepancy in my write-up rather than silently proceed as if it were already reconciled.", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "96a0105a-64a6-440a-96f7-9609c83b15f1"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:00:46Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 164, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Write", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01FpQsC9tJQWMNoZN6pyx4bh", "input": {"file_path": "/home/dev/projects/test-project/docs/design.md", "content": "# Design: Give tasks a priority\n\nSource: Taiga #4 spec text as provided in this dispatch (see note below).\n\n> **Note on `docs/spec.md`:** at the time of writing, `docs/spec.md` on disk\n> still holds the previous cycle's content (Taiga #3, \"Edit a task title\").\n> The product-manager's Taiga #4 spec was included directly in this\n> dispatch's \"What earlier stages produced\" section, pending the user's\n> confirmation to overwrite `docs/spec.md`/`docs/design.md` and to resolve\n> open question #1 (interleaved vs. full 3-tier sort). This design is built\n> against that inline text, under the same \"interleaved \u2014 only `high`\n> moves\" assumption the spec proceeds under. Recommend the orchestrator\n> confirm both points with the user and persist `docs/spec.md` before the\n> developer stage starts, so the developer isn't working from a stale file\n> on disk.\n\n## Summary of decisions\n\n- **One control does both jobs.** Rather than a read-only badge plus a\n  separate control to change priority (two elements fighting for space in\n  an already-tight row \u2014 see round 2's `Edit`/`Done`/`Delete` row), each\n  task gets a single native `<select>` \u2014 `priority-select` \u2014 that always\n  shows the current priority as its own visible text (\"Low\"/\"Normal\"/\n  \"High\") and lets the user change it in one action. This satisfies AC8\n  (visible at a glance) and the \"control to change priority without\n  entering the title-edit form\" requirement (spec open question 2) with no\n  new UI pattern beyond what the app already has (a styled `<button>`- and\n  `<input>`-adjacent form control).\n- **Color only on the two priorities that deviate from baseline.** `normal`\n  renders with the same neutral border/background every other control in\n  this app uses (`#ccd2d6` border, white fill) \u2014 it's the default, so it\n  shouldn't compete visually. `high` gets a solid warm-orange fill (new\n  accent, not a reuse of `.error`'s red \u2014 see \"Why not reuse the error\n  red\" below). `low` gets a light neutral-gray fill, visually recessive.\n  This means scanning the list, only `high` rows pull the eye, which is\n  literally the ask (\"Let me... see that at a glance\").\n- **Why not reuse the error red (`#a3271d`) for `high`:** round 2's design\n  established the rule \"one red = one meaning\" for this app \u2014 `#a3271d`\n  means \"something is wrong.\" Reusing it for \"high priority\" would make a\n  perfectly valid, successfully-saved task look like an error state.\n  Introducing one new accent (orange, a different hue from red) keeps that\n  semantic separation intact while still giving `high` a color no other\n  element in the app currently uses.\n- **Change applies immediately on select, no separate Save.** Matches this\n  app's two existing precedents for single-field, low-risk actions:\n  `TaskFilter`'s segmented control (click \u2192 immediately re-filters) and the\n  `Done` button (click \u2192 immediately marks complete). A priority change is\n  the same shape of action \u2014 one field, no risk of data loss, no reason to\n  gate it behind a confirm step the way title editing is (title editing\n  needs Save/Cancel because it's multi-character free text where a stray\n  edit is easy to want to undo; a 3-option select isn't).\n- **No optimistic update; follows the existing `run()` pattern exactly.**\n  `App.jsx`'s `run()` helper (lines 33\u201340) awaits the API call, then\n  `refresh()`s from the server, for every existing mutation (add, complete,\n  delete, edit-title). Priority change is wired the same way \u2014 no new\n  state-management pattern introduced. Practical effect: since the\n  `<select>`'s `value` prop is bound to `task.priority` (server state, not\n  local draft state), a failed change causes the control to simply snap\n  back to its last-known-good value on the next render, with the existing\n  global `.error` banner explaining why. No custom revert logic needed \u2014\n  it falls out of the existing controlled-component wiring for free.\n- **Sort is a stable partition applied after the existing filter step**, not\n  a general sort. `high` tasks float to the top in their original relative\n  order; everyone else (interleaved `normal`/`low`) keeps its original\n  relative order below them \u2014 per the spec's explicit \"without disturbing\n  the order of everything else\" and its open-question-1 assumption\n  (interleaved, only `high` moves). JS's `Array.prototype.sort` has been a\n  stable sort in every engine this project targets (Node/V8, all evergreen\n  browsers) since ES2019, so a boolean partition key (`priority === 'high'\n  ? 0 : 1`) is sufficient \u2014 no custom stable-sort implementation needed.\n- **`AddTask` gets the same `priority-select`, defaulted to `normal`.**\n  Reuses the exact same component/class as the row control rather than\n  inventing a second priority-picking pattern for creation vs. editing.\n\n## Component tree change\n\n```\nApp.jsx\n\u251c\u2500 AddTask.jsx\n\u2502   [title input] [priority-select, default \"Normal\"] [Add]   \u2190 select is NEW\n\u251c\u2500 TaskFilter.jsx (unchanged)\n\u2514\u2500 TaskList.jsx\n   \u2514\u2500 <li> per task (TaskRow)\n       [title] [priority-select]  [Edit] [Done] [Delete]      \u2190 select is NEW\n```\n\nNo new files, no new component library. `priority-select` is a shared CSS\nclass (not a new React component) applied to a plain `<select>` in both\n`AddTask.jsx` and `TaskList.jsx`'s `TaskRow`.\n\n## `AddTask.jsx` changes (shape, not full diff)\n\n```jsx\nconst PRIORITIES = [\n  { value: 'low', label: 'Low' },\n  { value: 'normal', label: 'Normal' },\n  { value: 'high', label: 'High' },\n]\n\nexport default function AddTask({ onAdd }) {\n  const [title, setTitle] = useState('')\n  const [priority, setPriority] = useState('normal')\n\n  function submit(e) {\n    e.preventDefault()\n    const trimmed = title.trim()\n    if (!trimmed) return\n    onAdd(trimmed, priority)\n    setTitle('')\n    setPriority('normal')\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <form className=\"add-task\" onSubmit={submit}>\n      <input\n        value={title}\n        onChange={(e) => setTitle(e.target.value)}\n        placeholder=\"What needs doing?\"\n        aria-label=\"Task title\"\n      />\n      <select\n        className={`priority-select priority-${priority}`}\n        value={priority}\n        onChange={(e) => setPriority(e.target.value)}\n        aria-label=\"Priority\"\n      >\n        {PRIORITIES.map((p) => <option key={p.value} value={p.value}>{p.label}</option>)}\n      </select>\n      <button type=\"submit\" disabled={!title.trim()}>Add</button>\n    </form>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`PRIORITIES` (value/label pairs) mirrors `TaskFilter.jsx`'s existing\n`OPTIONS` shape exactly \u2014 same pattern, new data.\n\n## `TaskList.jsx` changes (shape, not full diff)\n\nAdd a `priority-select` to `TaskRow`'s **view-mode** row only (edit mode \u2014\nthe title-editing form \u2014 is unaffected; priority stays visible and\neditable independently while a title edit is in progress, matching the\nspec's precedent that title-edit already leaves other fields alone):\n\n```jsx\nconst PRIORITIES = [\n  { value: 'low', label: 'Low' },\n  { value: 'normal', label: 'Normal' },\n  { value: 'high', label: 'High' },\n]\n\n// inside TaskRow(), view-mode return, before the Edit button:\n<select\n  className={`priority-select priority-${task.priority}`}\n  value={task.priority}\n  onChange={(e) => onPriorityChange(task.id, e.target.value)}\n  aria-label={`Priority for ${task.title}`}\n>\n  {PRIORITIES.map((p) => <option key={p.value} value={p.value}>{p.label}</option>)}\n</select>\n```\n\n`onPriorityChange` is a new prop threaded through `TaskList` \u2192\n`TaskRow`, alongside the existing `onComplete`/`onDelete`/`onUpdate`\n(`TaskList.jsx:9`, `:19-21`).\n\n`PRIORITIES` is duplicated between `AddTask.jsx` and `TaskList.jsx` as\nwritten above (2 short arrays, 3 entries each) rather than factored into a\nshared constants file \u2014 three lines twice is not worth a new module in a\nproject this size; if a third consumer shows up, that's the point to\nfactor it, not before.\n\n## `api.js` change\n\n```js\nexport const setPriority = (id, priority) =>\n  request(`/api/tasks/${id}/priority`, { method: 'PATCH', body: JSON.stringify({ priority }) })\n```\n\nMatches the existing `completeTask`/`updateTask` call shape\n(`api.js:19-21`). `createTask` extends to also send `priority` in its\nbody (exact signature is the developer's call \u2014 mirrors how `updateTask`\nalready takes `(id, title)`).\n\n## `App.jsx` changes (shape, not full diff)\n\n```jsx\nconst visibleTasks = tasks.filter((t) => {\n  if (filter === 'open') return !t.done\n  if (filter === 'done') return t.done\n  return true\n})\n\nconst sortedTasks = [...visibleTasks].sort(\n  (a, b) => (a.priority === 'high' ? 0 : 1) - (b.priority === 'high' ? 0 : 1)\n)\n```\n\n`sortedTasks` (not `visibleTasks`) is what gets passed to `<TaskList>`,\ndirectly after the existing filter step (`App.jsx:27-31`) \u2014 sort runs\n*within* the already-filtered subset, satisfying AC7 (a high-priority Done\ntask must not jump into the Open view).\n\n`AddTask`'s `onAdd` callback extends to accept `priority`:\n`onAdd={(title, priority) => run(() => api.createTask(title, priority))}`.\n\n`TaskList` gets a new prop:\n`onPriorityChange={(id, priority) => run(() => api.setPriority(id, priority))}`,\nwired identically to the existing `onComplete`/`onDelete`/`onUpdate` props\n(`App.jsx:51-56`).\n\n## `App.css` additions\n\n```css\n.priority-select {\n  padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;\n  border: 1px solid #ccd2d6;\n  border-radius: 5px;\n  background: #fff;\n  color: #16191c;\n  font: inherit;\n  font-size: 0.85rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n}\n\n.priority-select.priority-high {\n  background: #a8480a;\n  border-color: #a8480a;\n  color: #fff;\n}\n\n.priority-select.priority-low {\n  background: #e8eaec;\n  color: #4a545c;\n}\n\n/* .priority-normal intentionally has no override \u2014 it is the baseline\n   .priority-select styling above, so it blends into the row rather than\n   drawing the eye, same as everything that isn't flagged high. */\n```\n\n`.priority-select` base rule reuses the app's existing border color\n(`#ccd2d6`, already used by `.add-task input` and `.task-title-input`) and\nborder-radius (`5px`, used everywhere else in the file) \u2014 no new geometry\nintroduced. Approximate rendered height with this padding + 1px border +\ndefault line-height \u2248 30\u201332px, in line with the existing `Done`/`Delete`\nbuttons' ~34\u201336px (round 2's design note) \u2014 close enough not to look\nmismatched in the row, and clears the ~24px minimum target size guidance.\n\n## States\n\n### Populated \u2014 the spec's own AC5 example\n\nInput order `[A:normal, B:high, C:low, D:high, E:normal]`, Open filter (or\nAll), rendered order `[B, D, A, C, E]`:\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 B   [High\u25be]     [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2502 D   [High\u25be]     [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2502 A   [Normal\u25be]   [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2502 C   [Low\u25be]      [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2502 E   [Normal\u25be]   [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n`[High\u25be]` renders filled orange (`#a8480a` bg, white text); `[Low\u25be]` a\nlight gray chip (`#e8eaec` bg, `#4a545c` text); `[Normal\u25be]` plain\nwhite/bordered, indistinguishable from an ordinary form control. `B` and\n`D` keep their original relative order (`B` before `D`, as in the input);\n`A`, `C`, `E` keep theirs.\n\n### Populated \u2014 no `high` tasks present (AC6)\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 Buy milk        [Normal\u25be]   [Edit][Done][Delete]            \u2502\n\u2502 Walk the dog     [Low\u25be]     [Edit][Done][Delete]             \u2502\n\u2502 File taxes      [Normal\u25be]   [Edit][Done][Delete]            \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\nOrder is unchanged from today's id order \u2014 the sort key is `0`/`1` on\n\"is this `high`\", so with no `high` present every task ties at `1` and the\nstable sort leaves the array exactly as filtering produced it.\n\n### Populated \u2014 filter interaction (AC7)\n\nGiven the same 5-task set as above but with `B` marked `done`, viewing the\n**Open** filter: `B` (done) is excluded by the existing filter *before*\nthe priority sort runs, so it never appears \u2014 a high-priority Done task\ndoes not pull rows out of order in the Open view:\n\n```\nOpen filter:\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 D   [High\u25be]     [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2502 A   [Normal\u25be]   [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2502 C   [Low\u25be]      [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2502 E   [Normal\u25be]   [Edit][Done][Delete]                       \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n### Priority changed (AC9) \u2014 task leaves the top group\n\nBefore (D is `high`, sorted to top) \u2192 user selects \"Normal\" in D's row \u2192\n`onPriorityChange` fires \u2192 `run()` awaits the PATCH, then `refresh()`s \u2192\n`sortedTasks` recomputes on the next render, D falls back to its\nid-ordered position among the non-`high` rows:\n\n```\nBefore:                          After:\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510          \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 D  [High\u25be]           \u2502          \u2502 A  [Normal\u25be]         \u2502\n\u2502 A  [Normal\u25be]         \u2502    \u2192     \u2502 C  [Low\u25be]            \u2502\n\u2502 C  [Low\u25be]            \u2502          \u2502 D  [Normal\u25be]         \u2502\n\u2502 E  [Normal\u25be]         \u2502          \u2502 E  [Normal\u25be]         \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518          \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n### Creating a task with an explicit priority (AC2)\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 [ What needs doing?..............] [High\u25be] [Add]           \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n`AddTask`'s select defaults to `Normal` (AC1) on mount and resets to\n`Normal` after each successful add \u2014 same reset-after-submit behavior the\ntitle input already has (`setTitle('')`).\n\n### Error \u2014 priority change fails (network/server error)\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 \u26a0 Could not reach the server.                              \u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n\u2502 Buy milk        [Normal\u25be]   [Edit][Done][Delete]            \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\nSame global `.error` banner already used for every other failed mutation\nin this app (unchanged component). The select shows the task's actual\nlast-known-good priority (see \"No optimistic update\" above) rather than\nwhatever the user clicked, since it's bound to server state \u2014 no separate\nrevert logic to design or build.\n\n### Empty / Loading\n\nUnchanged. `.empty` (per-filter copy) and `.loading` states don't involve\nany task row, so they don't involve priority at all \u2014 no new design\nneeded here.\n\n## Interactive states\n\n- **`priority-select` closed, default (`normal`):** plain bordered control,\n  identical visual weight to `.task-title-input`.\n- **`priority-select` closed, `high`:** solid `#a8480a` fill, white text \u2014\n  the one state in a row designed to actively draw the eye.\n- **`priority-select` closed, `low`:** light `#e8eaec` fill, `#4a545c`\n  text \u2014 visually recessive, consistent with \"low\" meaning \"less urgent\n  than the baseline,\" not \"broken\" or \"hidden.\"\n- **`priority-select` open (dropdown expanded):** native OS/browser\n  rendering for the options list \u2014 not restyled. This app introduces no\n  custom-dropdown component anywhere else, and building one here would be\n  new UI-pattern surface the spec's non-goals don't ask for.\n- **`priority-select` focused:** native browser focus ring, same as every\n  other input/select in this app (no custom `:focus` rule exists\n  anywhere in `App.css` today \u2014 not introducing one here either).\n- **`priority-select` mid-request:** no per-control loading affordance,\n  consistent with round 2's explicit decision that this app has no\n  per-action loading state anywhere (`Done`/`Delete` don't show one\n  either) \u2014 only the shared error banner on failure.\n\n## Accessibility\n\n- **Labels:** `aria-label=\"Priority\"` on `AddTask`'s select,\n  `aria-label={\\`Priority for ${task.title}\\`}` on each row's select \u2014\n  same pattern already used for `Edit`/`Complete`/`Delete`\n  (`TaskList.jsx:74, 89, 91, 95`).\n- **Target size:** `.priority-select`'s padding (`0.4rem 0.6rem` + 1px\n  border) renders at roughly 30\u201332px tall, clearing the ~24px minimum and\n  sitting close to the row's existing `Edit`/`Done`/`Delete` buttons\n  (~34\u201336px per round 2's note) so it doesn't look like a stray smaller\n  control.\n- **Keyboard:** native `<select>` \u2014 full keyboard support (arrow keys to\n  change value, Enter/Space to open, no custom key handling needed). Tab\n  order in a row becomes `priority-select \u2192 Edit \u2192 Done \u2192 Delete`, visual\n  left-to-right, no `tabIndex` overrides, matching this app's existing \"no\n  tab-order overrides anywhere\" pattern.\n- **Color is not the only signal:** every state also shows its label text\n  (\"Low\"/\"Normal\"/\"High\") inside the control itself \u2014 color reinforces,\n  it doesn't carry the meaning alone. Satisfies WCAG 1.4.1 (use of color).\n- **Contrast \u2014 computed, not assumed:**\n\n  **`high`: white text `#ffffff` on `#a8480a` fill.**\n  - `#a8480a` \u2192 sRGB (0.6588, 0.2824, 0.0392)\n  - linearized: R \u2248 0.3914, G \u2248 0.0648, B \u2248 0.003035\n  - L = 0.2126\u00d70.3914 + 0.7152\u00d70.0648 + 0.0722\u00d70.003035 \u2248 0.1298\n  - white L = 1.0\n  - contrast = (1.0 + 0.05) / (0.1298 + 0.05) \u2248 **5.84:1** \u2014 passes the\n    4.5:1 text minimum with real margin (this is a first-pass color; an\n    earlier candidate, `#c2540d`, computed to 4.60:1 \u2014 too close to the\n    line for comfort, so I darkened it to `#a8480a` for headroom).\n  - Same figure covers the `border-color` (set equal to the fill) against\n    the white `.task` card background for the 3:1 non-text minimum \u2014 5.84\n    clears it easily.\n\n  **`low`: text `#4a545c` on `#e8eaec` fill.**\n  - `#e8eaec` (bg) \u2192 linearized R \u2248 0.8071, G \u2248 0.8228, B \u2248 0.8389 \u2192\n    L \u2248 0.8206\n  - `#4a545c` (text) \u2192 linearized R \u2248 0.0684, G \u2248 0.0886, B \u2248 0.1072 \u2192\n    L \u2248 0.0857\n  - contrast = (0.8206 + 0.05) / (0.0857 + 0.05) \u2248 **6.42:1** \u2014 passes\n    with margin.\n  - Contrast of the `#e8eaec` fill itself against the white `.task` card\n    background is only \u2248 **1.21:1** (L 0.8206 vs 1.0) \u2014 far under the 3:1\n    non-text minimum, meaning the chip's fill alone wouldn't read as a\n    distinct shape against a white card. That's why `.priority-select`'s\n    base rule already carries a `1px solid #ccd2d6` border \u2014 for `low`\n    this border isn't overridden, so it inherits that same border (already\n    \u22653:1 by existing precedent elsewhere in the file) and the chip stays\n    visually distinct by its outline even though the fill alone would not\n    be.\n  - `normal` needs no computation \u2014 it's the unmodified base style, already\n    in production use.\n\n## Acceptance criteria traceability\n\n| AC | Design element |\n|----|-----------------|\n| 1 | `AddTask`'s `priority-select` defaults to `normal` on mount/reset |\n| 2 | `priority-select` in `AddTask`, value sent via `onAdd(title, priority)` \u2192 `api.createTask` |\n| 3 | Row's `priority-select` `onChange` \u2192 `onPriorityChange(task.id, value)` \u2192 `api.setPriority`, only priority sent, mirrors title-edit precedent of touching one field |\n| 4 | Select only ever offers the 3 valid `<option>`s \u2014 invalid values aren't reachable via this UI; 422 is a backend defense-in-depth case (same precedent as title-edit's AC8) |\n| 5 | `sortedTasks` stable partition in `App.jsx`, applied after `visibleTasks`; \"Populated \u2014 AC5 example\" wireframe |\n| 6 | Partition key ties (`1`/`1`) when no `high` present \u2192 stable sort is a no-op; \"no `high` tasks\" wireframe |\n| 7 | Sort applied to `visibleTasks` (post-filter), not raw `tasks`; \"filter interaction\" wireframe |\n| 8 | `priority-select` always shows current value as visible text + color, no edit-mode required |\n| 9 | Sort recomputes on every render from `task.priority`; \"priority changed\" before/after wireframe |\n| 10 | Priority `<select>` and title-edit form are fully independent controls/state \u2014 editing one never touches the other, same as `done` already being independent of title today |\n\n## Non-goals honored\n\nNo new sort/filter UI control \u2014 the priority sort is automatic, exactly as\nscoped. No priority levels beyond low/normal/high \u2014 the `PRIORITIES` array\nhas exactly 3 entries. No bulk editing \u2014 each row's select is independent,\nsame as every other per-row control in this app. No change to `done`\nfiltering/sorting \u2014 the sort step runs strictly after the existing filter,\ntouches nothing about how `done` is computed or displayed. No priority\nhistory/audit trail \u2014 the select shows current value only, nothing new\npersisted about past values. No new UI library, icon set, or token\nsystem \u2014 one new CSS class family (`priority-select` + two modifiers)\nusing colors and geometry already established or freshly computed and\njustified above, no external dependency added.\n"}, "session_id": "96a0105a-64a6-440a-96f7-9609c83b15f1"}}
